+# Runtime diagnostic retention (isolated patch, not deployed)
+
+The existing `run_continuous_follow_logs/run_*` directories contain recordings,
+gallery material, and the complete high-volume event stream. They retain the
+existing latest-three-run policy. This patch does not change recording capture,
+motor behavior, serial timeouts, shutdown rules, or camera ownership.
+
+An independent `run_continuous_follow_diagnostics` pool now preserves useful
+failure evidence even after the original recording directory is pruned:
+
+- At most **7 days** from the run's original creation time and **20 MiB
+  (20,971,520 bytes)** of recognized diagnostic JSON files, including space
+  reserved for atomic replacement. Ordinary completed-run summaries are removed
+  before fault records; among faults, the oldest expires first. Incoming normal
+  summaries do not evict failures. Expiration is checked by the EventLog writer
+  at thread startup and archive writes; no independent daemon is installed.
+- The first fault keeps its structured `motor` snapshot, `traceback`, and event
+  timestamp. These include RTU register/function, TX/RX counts, timing, wheel
+  write stage, and STOP receipts **when the original event contains them**.
+  Later cleanup never overwrites the first fault, including after writer reopen.
+- A preceding window of at most 48 selected events / 256 KiB and up to 16
+  subsequent diagnostic/cleanup events provide context. It deliberately excludes
+  camera frames, target images, gallery samples, arbitrary stdout and terminal
+  command text. Secret keys and common credential text are redacted.
+- A single event is bounded to 512 KiB and a record to 2 MiB. Unusually large
+  events receive an explicit `archiveTruncated` marker instead of being presented
+  as complete; oversized context is marked `contextTruncated`. This does not
+  claim that unbounded original logs can be retained under a finite quota.
+- A new archive is written on the existing EventLog writer thread. `emit()` still
+  performs the original nonblocking queue operation. Slow/full/broken storage
+  cannot become a motor exception. Archive failure is exposed through
+  `EventLog.archive_error`, `diagnostic_archive_unavailable` in the main log, and
+  the final log summary. Atomic replacement failure preserves the earlier file.
+- An explicit offline helper can stream old recognized events files before
+  deleting their run directories. **The launcher does not opt into this scan**:
+  large old events files and slow disks must not delay follow startup. New runs
+  are archived in the writer thread instead. The administrative migration never
+  reads recordings; failure reports unavailability and retains the old bounded
+  recording-count policy. Already deleted details cannot be reconstructed.
+  Unknown files, symlinks, and unrecognized records are not followed/overwritten/
+  deleted as archive records.
+
+**Quota scope:** this new 20 MiB pool excludes the three raw recording directories
+and the preexisting `app_diagnostic_logs` session pool (which keeps its own
+existing quota). It is not a combined 20 MiB quota across all board files. The
+bounded diagnostics are correlated to session runId by their exact `runName`
+and the existing session `runtimeLogDir` field. No historical missing motor
+register values have been invented.
+
+Validation performed locally under WSL with temporary directories only:
+
+- 25 unittest cases (7 original preparation tests + 18 new archive tests) passed.
+- 9 original continuous-run filesystem/adoption tests passed. The one
+  configuration-check test was explicitly deselected because the isolated stage
+  does not contain the hardware/runtime dependency tree; no claim is made that
+  the complete runtime suite was run.
+- No SSH control command, motor/camera operation, service restart, or deployment
+  was performed while developing this patch.
